E = {
45: ("Conditions for wiping over khuffs", [
 "Seven conditions are required for wiping over khuffs to be permitted.",
 "First, putting them on after washing the feet, even before completing wudu, if he completes it before anything that breaks wudu happens.",
 "Second, that they cover the ankles.",
 "Third, that one can walk continuously in them; so it is not permitted over khuffs of glass, wood or iron.",
 "Fourth, that neither of them has a hole the size of three of the smallest toes.",
 "Fifth, that they stay on the feet without being tied.",
 "Sixth, that they prevent water from reaching the foot.",
 "Seventh, that there remains of the front of the foot the size of three of the smallest fingers. So if he has lost the front of his foot, he does not wipe on his khuff, even if the heel remains.",
]),
46: ("How long one may wipe", [
 "A resident wipes for a day and a night, and a traveller for three days and their nights.",
 "The period begins from the time of hadath after putting on the khuffs.",
 "If a resident wipes and then travels before his period ends, he completes the period of a traveller.",
 "If a traveller becomes resident after wiping for a day and a night, he takes them off; otherwise he completes a day and a night.",
]),
47: ("The obligatory amount of wiping", [
 "The obligatory wiping is the size of three of the smallest fingers on the top of the front of each foot.",
 "Its sunnah is to draw the fingers, spread apart, from the tips of the toes to the shin.",
]),
48: ("What breaks wiping over khuffs", [
 "Four things break the wiping over khuffs: everything that breaks wudu; taking off a khuff, even by most of the foot coming out into the leg of the khuff; water reaching most of one foot inside the khuff, according to the sound view; and the period ending, if he does not fear losing his foot to the cold.",
 "After the last three, he washes only his feet.",
]),
49: ("What may not be wiped over", ["It is not permitted to wipe over a turban, a cap, a face veil or gloves."]),
50: ("Splints and the like", [
 "If he has been bled, wounded or has broken a limb and bound it with a cloth or splint, and he cannot wash the limb or wipe it, he must wipe over most of what the limb is bound with; and for one who has been bled, wiping the visible skin between the bandage is enough.",
 "Wiping (over a splint) is like washing, so it has no time limit.",
 "The splint does not have to be put on in a state of purity. It is permitted to wipe the splint on one foot and wash the other. Wiping is not cancelled by the splint falling off before healing. It may be changed for another, and wiping over it again is not wajib, though it is better to repeat it.",
 "If his eye is inflamed and he has been told not to wash it, or his nail breaks and he puts medicine, gum or a piece of gall-bladder skin on it and removing it would harm him, he may wipe over it; and if wiping would harm him, he leaves it.",
]),
51: ("Intention in wiping", ["No intention is needed for wiping over khuffs, splints or the head."]),
52: ("Chapter: Menstruation, post-natal bleeding and irregular bleeding", ["What comes out of the private part is menstruation (hayd), post-natal bleeding (nifas) and irregular bleeding (istihadah)."]),
53: ("Menstruation", ["Menstruation is blood discharged by the womb of a woman who has reached puberty, who is not ill or pregnant, and who has not reached the age of menopause."]),
54: ("Its duration", ["The least menstruation is three days, the average five and the most ten."]),
55: ("Post-natal bleeding", ["Nifas is the blood that comes out after giving birth."]),
56: ("Its duration", ["Its maximum is forty days and it has no minimum."]),
57: ("Irregular bleeding", ["Istihadah is blood that is less than three days, or more than ten in menstruation, or more than forty in nifas."]),
58: ("The duration of purity", ["The least period of purity between two menstruations is fifteen days, and it has no maximum, except for one who has had istihadah since puberty."]),
59: ("What is forbidden during menstruation and nifas", ["Eight things are forbidden because of menstruation and nifas: prayer, fasting, reciting a verse of the Qur'an, touching it except with a separate cover, entering a masjid, tawaf, intercourse, and enjoyment of what is between the navel and below the knee."]),
60: ("When purity is complete", [
 "If the blood stops at the maximum of menstruation or nifas, intercourse becomes lawful without ghusl.",
 "If it stops before that, at the end of her habit, it is not lawful until she makes ghusl, or makes tayammum and prays, or a prayer becomes a debt upon her. That is, after the bleeding stops she has enough time in that prayer's time for ghusl and the opening takbir or more, and she does not make ghusl or tayammum until the time ends.",
]),
61: ("Making up obligations", ["A woman in menstruation or nifas makes up the fasts but not the prayers."]),
62: ("What is forbidden in janabah", ["Five things are forbidden in janabah: prayer, reciting a verse of the Qur'an, touching it except with a separate cover, entering a masjid, and tawaf."]),
63: ("What is forbidden for one without wudu", ["Three things are forbidden for one in minor hadath: prayer, tawaf, and touching the Mushaf except with a separate cover."]),
64: ("The ruling of istihadah and similar cases", [
 "The blood of istihadah is like a constant nosebleed: it does not prevent prayer, fasting or intercourse.",
 "A woman with istihadah, and anyone with an excuse such as constant urine or diarrhoea, makes wudu for the time of each obligatory prayer, and with it they may pray whatever obligatory and voluntary prayers they wish.",
 "The wudu of those with an excuse is broken only by the time ending.",
]),
65: ("When an excuse is established", [
 "One does not count as excused until the excuse lasts a whole prayer time without stopping for as long as it takes to make wudu and pray. This is the condition for it to be established.",
 "The condition for it to continue is that it happens in every prayer time after that, even once.",
 "The condition for it to end, so that the person is no longer excused, is a whole prayer time without it.",
]),
66: ("The kinds of impurity", [
 "Impurity is of two kinds: heavy (ghalizah) and light (khafifah).",
 "Heavy impurity is like wine, flowing blood, the flesh and untanned skin of an animal that died without slaughter, the urine of animals whose meat is not eaten, dog excrement, the droppings and saliva of predatory animals, the droppings of chickens, ducks and geese, and whatever breaks wudu when it comes out of the human body.",
 "Light impurity is like the urine of a horse, likewise the urine of animals whose meat is eaten, and the droppings of birds that are not eaten.",
]),
67: ("Impurity that is overlooked", [
 "The size of a dirham of heavy impurity is overlooked, and less than a quarter of the garment or body of light impurity; and splashes of urine like the tips of needles are overlooked.",
 "If an impure bed or earth becomes wet from a sleeper's sweat or a wet foot, and a trace of the impurity appears on the body or foot, they become impure; otherwise not.",
 "Likewise, a dry pure garment wrapped in a wet impure garment does not become impure if no liquid would drip from it if wrung. A wet garment does not become impure by being spread on dry impure ground that becomes damp from it, nor from wind blowing over impurity onto the garment, unless its trace appears in it.",
]),
68: ("How impurity is purified", [
 "Something with visible impurity becomes pure when the substance is removed, even with one washing, according to the sound view; a trace that is hard to remove does no harm. Invisible impurity is purified by washing three times and wringing each time.",
 "Impurity is removed from garments and the body with water and with any liquid that removes it, such as vinegar and rose water.",
 "A khuff and the like is purified by rubbing it from impurity that has body, even if it is wet.",
 "A sword and the like is purified by wiping.",
]),
69: ("Purifying the ground", ["When the trace of impurity has gone from the ground and it has dried, prayer on it is permitted, but not tayammum with it. Trees and standing grass on it become pure by drying."]),
70: ("Purification by transformation", ["Impurity becomes pure when its substance is transformed, such as becoming salt or being burnt by fire. Dry semen is purified from garments and the body by rubbing it off, and wet semen by washing it."]),
71: ("The skin of animals that died without slaughter", [
 "The skin of an animal that died is purified by real tanning, such as with qaraz, and by tanning in effect, such as with dust and sun, except the skin of the pig and of a human.",
 "Lawful slaughter purifies the skin of an animal that is not eaten, but not its flesh, according to the soundest view on which fatwa is given. Anything in which blood does not flow does not become impure by death, such as hair, cut feathers, horn, hooves and bone without grease on it. Nerve is impure according to the sound view. The musk pod is pure like musk, and eating it is lawful. Civet is pure, and the prayer of one perfumed with it is valid.",
]),
72: ("Conditions that make prayer obligatory", [
 "Three things are conditions for prayer to be fard: Islam, puberty and sanity.",
 "Children are told to pray at seven, and smacked for it at ten, with the hand and not a stick.",
]),
73: ("Its cause and when it becomes due", ["Its causes are its times; it becomes due at the start of the time, with room to delay."]),
74: ("The prayer times", [
 "The times are five.",
 "Fajr: from the rise of the true dawn until just before sunrise.",
 "Zuhr: from the sun passing its zenith until the shadow of everything is twice its length, or equal to it, not counting the shadow at the zenith. Al-Tahawi chose the second, which is the view of the two companions (Abu Yusuf and Muhammad رحمهما الله).",
 "'Asr: from when the shadow passes one length or two lengths until sunset.",
 "Maghrib: from sunset until the red twilight disappears, according to the position on which fatwa is given.",
 "'Isha' and witr: from then until Fajr. Witr is not prayed before 'Isha', because of the required order.",
 "Whoever does not find their time (where the twilight does not disappear) is not obliged to pray them.",
]),
75: ("Combining two obligatory prayers", ["Two obligatory prayers are not combined in one time because of an excuse, except at 'Arafah for the pilgrim, on condition of the supreme Imam and ihram: he combines Zuhr and 'Asr, praying 'Asr early. Maghrib and 'Isha' are combined at Muzdalifah, and Maghrib is not valid on the road to Muzdalifah."]),
76: ("The recommended times of prayer", ["It is recommended for men to pray Fajr when the light has spread; to delay Zuhr in summer until the heat lessens and to pray it early in winter, except on a cloudy day, when it is delayed; to delay 'Asr as long as the sun has not changed, and to pray it early on a cloudy day; to pray Maghrib early, except on a cloudy day, when it is delayed; to delay 'Isha' to a third of the night and pray it early when cloudy; and to delay witr to the end of the night for one who is confident of waking."]),
77: ("Times when prayer is invalid or disliked", [
 "There are three times in which no fard or wajib prayer that became due before them is valid: from sunrise until the sun has risen high; when it is at its zenith until it passes it; and when it turns yellow until it sets.",
 "What became due within those times may be performed in them with dislike, such as a funeral that arrives then, or a prostration for a verse recited then; just as the 'Asr of that day is valid at sunset with dislike.",
 "In these three times voluntary prayer is prohibitively disliked, even if it has a cause, such as a vowed prayer or the two rak'ahs of tawaf.",
]),
78: ("When voluntary prayer is disliked", ["Voluntary prayer is disliked after dawn beyond its sunnah; after the Fajr prayer; after the 'Asr prayer; before the Maghrib prayer; when the khatib comes out until the prayer is finished; when the iqamah is called, except the sunnah of Fajr; before 'Id, even at home, and after it in the masjid; between the two combined prayers at 'Arafah and Muzdalifah; when the time of the obligatory prayer is short; when holding back the call of nature; when food is present that one craves; and with anything that occupies the mind and spoils humility."]),
79: ("The ruling on the adhan and iqamah", ["The adhan and iqamah are an emphasised sunnah for the obligatory prayers, even for one praying alone, on time or making up, travelling or resident, for men; and they are disliked for women."]),
80: ("How they are made", [
 "He says the takbir four times at its start, and the takbir at its end twice, like the rest of its words. There is no tarji' (repeating) in the two testimonies.",
 "The iqamah is like it, adding after the hayya 'ala'l-falah of Fajr \"Prayer is better than sleep\" twice, and after the hayya 'ala'l-falah of the iqamah \"The prayer has begun\" twice. He goes slowly in the adhan and quickly in the iqamah.",
]),
81: ("The adhan in a language other than Arabic", ["It is not valid in Persian, even if it is known to be the adhan, according to the clearer view."]),
82: ("What is recommended for the mu'adhdhin and in the adhan", [
 "It is recommended that the mu'adhdhin is righteous, knows the sunnah and the prayer times, is in wudu, and faces the qiblah unless he is riding; that he puts his fingers in his ears; that he turns his face right at \"the prayer\" and left at \"success\"; that he turns around in his minaret; and that he separates the adhan and iqamah by enough time for the regular worshippers to come, while keeping to the recommended time; and in Maghrib by a pause the length of three short verses or three steps.",
 "He may make tathwib, such as saying after the adhan: \"The prayer, the prayer, O you who pray.\"",
]),
83: ("What is disliked in them", ["Melodising is disliked; and the iqamah and adhan of one without wudu; the adhan of one in janabah, a child without understanding, an insane person, a drunk, a woman, a sinner, or one sitting; and talking during the adhan and the iqamah. It is recommended to repeat the adhan (in these cases), but not the iqamah. Both are disliked for Zuhr on Friday in a town (for those excused from Jumu'ah)."]),
84: ("The adhan for missed prayers", ["One makes the adhan and iqamah for a missed prayer, and likewise for the first of several missed prayers. For the rest, leaving the iqamah is disliked but not leaving the adhan, if they are made up in one sitting."]),
85: ("What to say on hearing the mu'adhdhin", ["When one hears the sunnah adhan, one stops and repeats it; at the two hayya 'ala one says la hawla wa la quwwata illa billah; and when the mu'adhdhin says \"Prayer is better than sleep\" one says \"You have spoken truly and well\", or ma sha' Allah. Then one asks for the wasilah, saying: \"O Allah, Lord of this perfect call and the prayer about to be established, grant our master Muhammad the wasilah and excellence, and raise him to the praised station that You have promised him.\""]),
86: ("What is necessary for prayer to be valid", [
 "Twenty-seven things are necessary for the prayer to be valid:",
 "purity from hadath; purity of the body, clothes and place from impurity that is not overlooked, including where the feet, hands, knees and forehead are placed, according to the soundest view; covering the 'awrah, and it does no harm if it can be seen through one's collar or from below one's hem; facing the qiblah: for a Makkan who can see it, his obligation is to face the Ka'bah itself, and for one who cannot see it, its direction, even in Makkah, according to the sound view; the time; believing it has come in; the intention; the opening takbir without a gap; saying the opening takbir standing before bending for ruku'; not delaying the intention after the opening takbir; saying the takbir so that one hears oneself, according to the soundest view; the follower intending to follow; specifying the obligatory prayer; specifying the wajib prayer, while specifying is not required for voluntary prayer; standing, except in voluntary prayer; recitation, even one verse, in two rak'ahs of the obligatory prayer and in every rak'ah of voluntary prayer and witr, and no particular part of the Qur'an is required for validity, and the follower does not recite but listens attentively, and if he recites it is prohibitively disliked; ruku'; prostrating on something whose firmness one feels and on which the forehead rests, even on one's palm or the edge of one's clothing if the place it is on is pure, prostrating as a wajib with the hard part of the nose and with the forehead, and limiting it to the nose is not valid except for an excuse affecting the forehead; the place of prostration not being higher than the place of the feet by more than half a cubit, and if it is more, the prostration is not valid, except in a crowd where one prostrates on the back of someone praying the same prayer; placing the hands and knees, according to the sound view, and some of the toes on the ground while prostrating, and placing the top of the foot is not enough; ruku' coming before prostration; rising from the prostration to nearly sitting, according to the soundest view; returning to the (second) prostration; the final sitting for the length of the tashahhud, coming after the pillars; performing them while awake; knowing how the prayer is done and which of its parts are obligatory, in a way that distinguishes them from the sunnah parts; and believing that it is fard, so that he does not perform an obligation as though it were voluntary.",
]),
87: ("The pillars of prayer", ["The pillars among these are four: standing, recitation, ruku' and prostration. It is said that the final sitting for the length of the tashahhud is also one."]),
88: ("The conditions of prayer", ["The rest are conditions: some are conditions for validly beginning the prayer, those that are outside it; and the others are conditions for it to remain valid."]),
89: ("Purity of the place", ["It is permitted to pray on felt whose top side is pure and bottom side impure; on a pure cloth with an impure lining if they are not stitched together; and on a pure edge (of a cloth), even if the impure edge moves when he moves, according to the sound view."]),
90: ("Purity of clothing", [
 "If one end of his turban becomes impure and he throws it off and keeps the pure part on his head, and the impure end does not move when he moves, his prayer is permitted; if it moves, it is not.",
 "One who has nothing with which to remove impurity prays with it and need not repeat the prayer.",
]),
91: ("Covering the 'awrah", ["Nor (does he repeat) if he has nothing to cover his 'awrah with, not even silk, grass or mud. If he finds something, even by its being made available to him, and a quarter of it is pure, his prayer naked is not valid. If less than a quarter is pure, he may choose; and his praying in a garment that is entirely impure is more beloved than praying naked. If he finds something that covers part of the 'awrah, he must use it, covering the front and back private parts; if it covers only one, it is said he covers the back and it is said the front."]),
92: ("The prayer of one without clothing", ["It is recommended for one without clothing to pray sitting, using gestures, with his legs stretched towards the qiblah. If he prays standing with gestures, or with ruku' and prostration, it is valid."]),
93: ("The 'awrah", ["A man's 'awrah is between the navel and the end of the knee. A slave-woman's includes in addition the stomach and back. The whole body of a free woman is 'awrah except her face, palms and feet."]),
94: ("Uncovering it", ["Uncovering a quarter of one limb of the 'awrah prevents the prayer from being valid. If the uncovering is spread over several limbs of the 'awrah and together it reaches a quarter of the smallest of the uncovered limbs, it also prevents it; otherwise not."]),
95: ("Facing the qiblah", [
 "One who cannot face the qiblah because of illness, or cannot get down from his mount, or fears an enemy, faces whatever direction he is able to and is safe in.",
 "One who is unsure of the qiblah, with no one to tell him and no mihrab, uses his best judgement, and does not repeat the prayer if he was wrong. If he learns he was wrong during the prayer, he turns and continues. If he began without using his judgement and learns after finishing that he was right, it is valid; if he learns during it that he was right, it is invalid, as it is if he never learns whether he was right. If a group each use their judgement and face different directions, not knowing their imam's direction, it is valid for them.",
]),
96: ("The wajib acts of prayer", [
 "They are eighteen:",
 "reciting al-Fatihah; adding a surah or three verses in two unspecified rak'ahs of the obligatory prayer and in every rak'ah of witr and voluntary prayer; specifying the first two rak'ahs for recitation; reciting al-Fatihah before the surah; adding the nose to the forehead in prostration; making the second prostration in each rak'ah before moving to anything else; calmness in the pillars; the first sitting; reciting the tashahhud in it, according to the sound view; reciting it in the final sitting; standing up for the third rak'ah without delay after the tashahhud; the word al-salam, without 'alaykum; the qunut of witr; the takbirs of the two 'Ids; specifically the takbir for opening each prayer, not only in the 'Ids; the takbir for ruku' in the second rak'ah of the two 'Ids; the imam reciting aloud in Fajr, the first two rak'ahs of Maghrib and 'Isha', even when made up, Jumu'ah, the two 'Ids, tarawih and witr in Ramadan; and reciting silently in Zuhr and 'Asr, after the first two rak'ahs of Maghrib and 'Isha', and in daytime voluntary prayers. One praying alone may choose in the prayers recited aloud, like one praying voluntary prayers at night.",
 "If he left out the surah in the first two rak'ahs of 'Isha', he recites it in the last two with al-Fatihah, aloud. If he left out al-Fatihah, he does not repeat it in the last two.",
]),
97: ("The sunnahs of prayer", [
 "They are fifty-one:",
 "raising the hands for the opening takbir to the level of the ears for a man and a slave-woman, and to the shoulders for a free woman; spreading the fingers; the follower's opening takbir coinciding with his imam's; a man placing his right hand on his left below his navel, the manner being to put the inside of the right palm on the back of the left, ringing the wrist with the little finger and thumb; a woman placing her hands on her chest without ringing; the opening praise (thana'); seeking refuge for the recitation; saying bismillah at the start of each rak'ah; saying amin; the tahmid; saying these silently; standing straight at the opening takbir without lowering the head; the imam saying the takbir and tasmi' aloud; spacing the feet four fingers apart while standing; the surah added to al-Fatihah being from the long mufassal in Fajr and Zuhr, the middle mufassal in 'Asr and 'Isha', and the short mufassal in Maghrib, if resident, while a traveller recites whatever he wishes; lengthening the first rak'ah in Fajr only; the takbir for ruku'; its tasbih three times; holding the knees with the hands; spreading the fingers, while a woman does not; keeping the shins upright; flattening the back; levelling the head with the hips; rising from ruku' and standing calmly after it; placing the knees, then the hands, then the face for prostration, and the reverse when rising; the takbir of prostration and the takbir of rising; prostrating between the palms; its tasbih three times; a man keeping his stomach away from his thighs, his elbows from his sides and his forearms from the ground; a woman lowering herself and keeping her stomach against her thighs; standing up straight (after ruku'); the sitting between the two prostrations; placing the hands on the thighs between the two prostrations as in the tashahhud; sitting on the left foot and keeping the right upright; a woman sitting on her hip; pointing with the index finger at the testimony, according to the sound view, raising it at the negation and lowering it at the affirmation; reciting al-Fatihah after the first two rak'ahs; sending blessings on our master the Prophet ﷺ in the final sitting; supplicating with words like those of the Qur'an and Sunnah, not people's speech; turning right then left with the two salams; the imam intending the men, the recording angels and the righteous jinn with the two salams, according to the soundest view; the follower intending his imam in his direction, and if he is directly behind him, intending him in both salams along with the people, the recording angels and the righteous jinn; one praying alone intending only the angels; making the second salam quieter than the first; making it together with the imam's salam; beginning with the right; and a late-comer waiting for the imam to finish.",
]),
98: ("The manners of prayer", ["Among its manners: a man bringing his hands out of his sleeves at the takbir; looking at the place of prostration while standing, the top of the feet in ruku', the tip of the nose in prostration, the lap while sitting, and the shoulders at the salam; suppressing coughing as much as possible; closing the mouth when yawning; standing when \"hayya 'ala'l-falah\" is said; and the imam beginning once \"qad qamat al-salah\" is said."]),
99: ("How the prayer is put together", [
 "When a man wishes to begin the prayer, he brings his hands out of his sleeves, then raises them to the level of his ears, then says the takbir without lengthening it, with the intention. Beginning is valid with any remembrance purely for Allah the Exalted, such as subhan Allah, and in Persian if he is unable to say it in Arabic; but if he is able, beginning in Persian is not valid, nor reciting in it, according to the soundest view. Then he places his right hand on his left below his navel straight after the opening takbir without pause, and recites the opening praise: \"Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise; blessed is Your name and exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god but You.\" Everyone praying recites it. Then he seeks refuge for the recitation, which the late-comer says but not the follower, and it is said after the takbirs of the 'Ids. Then he says bismillah silently, in every rak'ah before al-Fatihah only. Then he recites al-Fatihah, and the imam and follower say amin silently. Then he recites a surah or three verses. Then he says the takbir and bows calmly, levelling his head with his hips, holding his knees with his hands with his fingers spread, and says the tasbih three times, which is the least. Then he raises his head, standing calmly, saying \"Allah hears the one who praises Him; our Lord, to You is praise\" if imam or alone, while the follower says only the tahmid. Then he says the takbir and goes down to prostrate, placing his knees, then his hands, then his face between his palms, and prostrates on his nose and forehead calmly, saying the tasbih three times, which is the least. He keeps his stomach away from his thighs and his upper arms away from his armpits when it is not crowded, with the fingers of his hands and feet pointing towards the qiblah; a woman lowers herself and keeps her stomach against her thighs. Then he raises his head with the takbir and sits between the two prostrations with his hands on his thighs, calmly. Then he says the takbir and prostrates calmly, says the tasbih three times, keeps his stomach away from his thighs and his upper arms away from his sides. Then he raises his head with the takbir to stand up, without leaning on the ground with his hands and without sitting.",
 "The second rak'ah is like the first, except that he does not say the opening praise or seek refuge.",
]),
}
